lane: follower-sees-appends
why: a SECOND handle on one journal directory never sees what the first
  appends. `Part` keeps each segment's base/count/size in memory from
  the moment it opened, and `read` decides where the active segment ends
  from `seg.count`, so a reader in another process tails nothing — it
  also never notices a segment the writer rolled. specs/persist.md ticks
  "poll-on-end" and the module's own header calls several processes on
  one shared log "the arrangement this module's two-node story
  describes"; both are true only within one handle. Found by okay-chat
  building a reading replica (its BUGS.md follower-cannot-see-new-
  records): the follower applied 0 records while the log had grown by
  four, and it now reopens the journal every poll to work around it.
how: the length of the active segment is a property of the FILE. `read`
  refreshes the active segment from the file (remap, scan on from the
  last valid end — the scan already validates length and CRC, so a torn
  tail stops it exactly as it does on recovery); when that yields
  nothing and the caller is at the end, it looks at the DIRECTORY for a
  segment rolled since. Two tests at the platform level: one handle
  writes, another reads, across a roll.
